WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.270
This episode was made possible by generous
supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.270
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:11.870
Let me tell you, light is crazy fast.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:15.860
It travels at 671 million miles per hour.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.900
That’s 186,000 miles per second

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.560
or 300,000 kilometers per second

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.320
or 30 centimeters nanosecond!

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.640
Whatever units you choose, that’s crazy
fast.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:28.460
Fast Fast!

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:30.780
So fast, it might leave you wondering:

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.460
How fast is that exactly?

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.220
Let’s do some comparisons!

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:41.460
The fastest humans in history were the Apollo
10 astronauts at 0.006% the SOL.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:48.180
The fastest human-made object was the Juno
space probe at 0.025% the SOL.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.560
Andromeda is hurtling toward the Milky Way
Galaxy at 0.04% the SOL.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.660
All of these are tiny fractions of that 671
million mph.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.540
Physicists have only really gotten close to the
speed of light with subatomic particles.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:13.200
The record for a proton in the Large Hadron
Collider is 99.999 999% the SOL.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.560
Now that’s fast!

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.440
Fast Fast!

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.920
I’m a mad scientist though.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.160
We can do better!

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.380
I have a specially designed rocket just for
this purpose.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.640
This seems dangerous.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.660
I probably shouldn’t do this myself.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.340
We’ll use Rocket Clone.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.320
Oh, right.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.640
We lost him during the Twins Paradox video.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.840
Guess I’ll have to make another one.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.980
[Machine Noises]

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.360
Alright, now we have a Rocket Clone. Let’s do this.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.380
Say he gradually accelerates away from my
space station.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.900
Faster and faster and faster.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.800
How long will it take him to reach light speed?

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.220
Well, he won’t.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.660
See, accelerating a rest mass like a person
or a rocket requires energy.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:13.099
For example, a rocket transforms energy from
its fuel into kinetic energy.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:16.579
It will continue to accelerate as long as
it burns fuel.

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:21.609
Even if he somehow managed to turn all of
the energy in the observable universe

00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:27.620
into kinetic energy for himself,
he’d still only be going 99.999...

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.880
That’s 100 nines after the decimal place,
which is still not 100%.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.279
It would literally take an infinite amount
of energy over an infinite amount of time

00:02:36.279 --> 00:02:38.969
to accelerate up to exactly the speed of light.

00:02:38.969 --> 00:02:40.160
It’s impossible.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.859
That being said, there are things in the universe
that go at exactly light speed.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.140
Namely, light.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.720
And that’s any light from the spectrum,
not just visible light.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.060
But also gravitational waves and anything
else without rest mass.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:53.980
Gluons.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.239
Anyway, that speed does exist,
but the fact that it’s impossible for massive

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.160
objects to reach it has a weird side effect.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.660
No matter how fast you go, light will always
be faster.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.680
671 million mph faster.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.700
Light is so fast that it breaks our understanding
of how speed works.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.480
How can it always be going the same speed
faster than you?

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.000
Because space-time.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.840
We’re going to need a space-time diagram.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.960
If you’re not familiar with those, you should
go check out this video first.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.820
Alright, let’s bring back Rocket Clone for
another experiment.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:31.499
If, instead of accelerating, he travels at
steady speed his space-time path will look

00:03:31.499 --> 00:03:32.499
like this.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.300
Let’s consider the part between my space
station and, oh I don’t know, Wolf 359.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.560
They’re about 8 light years apart and the
trip takes about 14.5 years.

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:47.810
We know speed is space divided by time,
so 8 light years divided by 14.5 years is

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:51.680
0.55 light,
or 55% the speed of light.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:57.040
We can draw those measurements as a triangle,
which makes the speed the tangent of an angle,

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.160
but not the tangent you know and love.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.520
Most people don’t love trigonometry.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.440
Really?

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.560
Yes, really.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.700
Weird.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.060
Anyway, this isn’t a regular tangent.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.319
It’s a hyperbolic tangent or “tanch”
for short.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.340
Switching from my point of view to Rocket
Clone’s is a hyperbolic rotation.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.140
Don’t ever forget Relativity Rule #3.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:18.780
Huh?

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.120
Let me just show you.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.940
These are my coordinates.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.740
These are Rocket Clone’s.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:24.720
Me.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.920
Rocket Clone.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:26.800
Me.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.040
Rocket Clone.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.140
The space and time axes rotate in opposite
directions

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.020
to line up with the observer taking the measurements.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.400
That’s called a hyperbolic rotation
because the coordinates on each axis

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.260
move along hyperbolas.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:44.740
The more his path is tipped from mine
the faster he’s moving according to me.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.780
So let’s look at a bunch of different speeds
and see what happens.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.280
This is stationary.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.900
This is 10% the SOL.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.680
Here’s 25%

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:54.960
50%

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.780
75%

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:57.840
90

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.160
99

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:02.020
99.9999

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:03.600
Seeing a pattern here?

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.130
They’re approaching this diagonal path.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:09.340
Even the accelerated rocket from earlier approaches
it.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.780
That diagonal is the path that light would
take in space-time.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.910
At least, if it was traveling the same direction.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:18.970
Both this rocket and this light are traveling
to the right.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:22.320
If we sent another set to the left, it would
look like this.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.260
The angles in here are weird though.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:27.560
These two angles give us 55% the speed of light,

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.660
but this angle only gives us 84.5%, not 110%.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.660
These two angles give us exactly the speed
of light

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.400
and this angle also gives us exactly the speed
of light.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.980
Any angle measured from those diagonal paths
will give you exactly the speed of light.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.340
Every single one of these angles gives us
exactly the speed of light.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:50.990
That is some crazy distortion!!

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.240
The point is that a hyperbolic rotation keeps
the speed of light constant.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:01.160
The speed of light is measured to be exactly
the same by everyone, no matter what.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.620
So here’s what we’ve learned:

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:06.400
The speed of light is the upper limit for speed in any direction.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.200
It’s 671 million mph, which is unimaginably
fast.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.260
So fast, that no matter how fast you go, light
will always be faster.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.700
671 million mph faster!!

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.860
Just in case this hasn’t completely sunk
into your brains yet.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:32.600
Even if you’re going 670 million mph, light
is still going 671 million mph faster than you.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.020
That’s crazy!!

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.060
So, how crazy do you think light is?

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.300
Let us know in the comments.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.860
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:41.420
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like
to keep up with us.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:45.450
A special thanks goes out to Patreon patrons
like Ilya Yashin and Drake Dragon

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.240
for helping make this show possible.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.780
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.320
For those of you saying:
You think the acceleration still matters for

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:56.560
the Twins Paradox.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.260
Well, yeah, sure, it matters,
just not in the way people usually says that it does.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:05.620
The acceleration doesn’t suddenly explain
time differences between observers.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.310
It just makes it possible for them to compare
notes.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.140
Anyway, thanks for watching!

